@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+# The Daily Grind
+
+Run `npm install`, then `npm start` (default port 11222; override with `PORT`).
+`npm run build` compiles the strict TypeScript server and client and copies the public assets into `dist/public`. Run `npm test` for policy, HTTP integration and client error-handling checks.
+
+Serve through the included server to deliver the required HTTP headers. If deploying behind a proxy, preserve `Connection-Allowlist`, `Reporting-Endpoints`, `Content-Security-Policy` and `Vary`. Static hosting must configure equivalent HTTP response headers; the HTML CSP meta element provides only document-level fallback restrictions.
+
+The policy permits the response origin and the existing Unsplash image origin. Redirects and WebRTC stay blocked by default. Scripts and styles are local files so the CSP needs no inline exceptions. Dedicated, shared and service worker requests receive the same policy as documents, a superset of every document policy. There are currently no worker scripts or WebSocket connections in the app.
+
+Set `CONNECTION_POLICY_MODE=report-only` to audit the connection allowlist during rollout. CSP remains enforced as a fallback. The policy helper also supports a simultaneous stricter `reportOnlyPatterns` candidate. The same-origin reporting endpoint accepts bounded JSON report batches and returns 204; connect it to your reporting storage for durable analysis. Reports are not persisted or logged by this demo server.
+
+Use the connection status buttons to check a same-origin request and a blocked third-party request. Connection-Allowlist is browser enforced and cannot be polyfilled; CSP supplies fetch restrictions in browsers without support. Client errors cover both policy rejection and network failure, since fetch cannot reliably distinguish them. Allowed external requests are still subject to CORS and endpoint availability.
